<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Sakinah frosted-glass surface tokens.

      Alpha only. No gradients, no shadows, no rim lights. Depth is the real window
      blur (helper/GlassBlur.kt) plus one step of tint per level, and that is all.

      WHY PURE ALPHA, NOT FIXED COLOURS
      A launcher draws over an arbitrary wallpaper, so any token pinned to a specific
      hex is only correct for one wallpaper. Every value here is a translucent
      black-or-white wash, which means each level is defined by its RELATIONSHIP to
      whatever is behind it. The previous iteration used absolute values (#26262E,
      #33333D...) tuned against one emulator wallpaper; they measured beautifully there
      and inverted on a bright wallpaper.

      THE RULE
      A panel is tinted with the INVERSE of its ink, and each level moves one step
      further from the ink:
        dark theme  = white ink -> scrim darkens, wells darken more, cards lift slightly
        light theme = black ink -> scrim lightens, wells darken, cards lighten more
      Getting this backwards is what made "glass" panels invisible: a white-tinted
      panel under white text has no contrast to give.

      VERIFIED (uitest/contrast_probe.py, uitest/depth_probe.py, both themes, on-device)
      Worst case is the wallpaper that fights the theme — pure white behind a dark
      theme, pure black behind a light one — and, for the light theme, also pure white,
      where a white scrim saturates:
        dark  : ink on well 10.7:1, ink on card 4.7:1, card/pane 1.32, well/pane 1.72
        light : ink on well  8.1:1, ink on card 13.6:1, card/pane 1.36, well/pane 1.23
    -->

    <!-- Light theme: black ink -> light panels.

         The scrim and cards are WHITE washes; the well is a light BLACK wash. That
         asymmetry is deliberate and both alternatives were measured and rejected:

           * all-white ladder (well also white, just less of it) SATURATES. On a white
             wallpaper the scrim already reaches 255, so pane, well and card all land on
             the same value and nothing separates but the hairline.
           * a heavy black well (the mirror of the dark theme) reads as a grubby grey
             patch on an otherwise clean panel, not as depth.

         10% black keeps the well 1.23-1.25:1 below both the pane and any card it sits
         inside, on every wallpaper from black to white, while black ink on it still
         scores at least 9.0:1.

         The scrim is heavier than the dark theme's (86% vs 62%) and that asymmetry is
         the point. Behind a panel the wallpaper is blurred and crushed towards mid-grey,
         so at 70% the "light" pane measured luminance 0.475 on device: a grey card, not
         a light sheet. 86% brings it to 0.69, which reads as paper. A dark theme has no
         equivalent problem because crushing towards grey is already halfway to where it
         wants to be. -->
    <color name="scrimOnLight">#DBFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="glassSunkenOnLight">#1A000000</color>
    <color name="glassRaisedOnLight">#61FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- Near-opaque ink. Anything drawn on this MUST flip to primaryInverseColor:
         see helper/GlassInk.kt and @style/TextOnGlassActive. Not pure black: at full
         strength the selected chip was the only hard-edged element on an otherwise
         soft panel and read as a hole. -->
    <color name="glassActiveOnLight">#CC000000</color>
    <color name="hairlineOnLight">#3D000000</color>

    <!-- Dark theme: white ink -> dark panels. -->
    <color name="scrimOnDark">#9E000000</color>
    <color name="glassSunkenOnDark">#5B000000</color>
    <color name="glassRaisedOnDark">#1EFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="glassActiveOnDark">#E6FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="hairlineOnDark">#3DFFFFFF</color>

    <!--
      HOME LEGIBILITY — for text drawn straight on the wallpaper. Separate from the panel
      scrim; see values/attrs.xml for why.

      Split into two surfaces because the clock and the app labels want opposite things:

        homeWashTop   62%  the clock band (drawable/bg_home_clock_wash.xml), 220dp tall,
                           fading to transparent. Sized to the text, so the wallpaper
                           below is untouched. At a flatter 45% the clock measured
                           3.94:1 on this wallpaper and looked washed out.
        homeWashFloor 45%  flat, full-height (drawable/bg_home_wash.xml), for the app
                           labels in the middle of the screen. This is the floor, chosen
                           by arithmetic rather than by eye: against a wallpaper that
                           fights the theme, the wash is the ONLY thing separating a
                           label from its background. An idealised calculation said 35%
                           was enough, but measuring a real glyph on device gave a core
                           luminance of 0.016 rather than 0 (antialiasing never reaches
                           the pure ink colour), and at that value 35% is 2.28:1. 45%
                           measures 3.34:1 on both sides. @style/TextHomeLabel's halo is
                           on top of that, not instead of it.

      Measured on this wallpaper (uitest/out/hw5_yes.png): luminance is ~0.22 across the
      top third and 0.003 below it, so the bright region worth defending against really
      is the clock's own band. An earlier attempt served both from ONE full-height 3-stop
      gradient and the geometry defeats it — a linear gradient's middle stop sits at the
      middle of the VIEW, so the clock's band ended up dimming the whole top half.

      KNOWN TRADE-OFF, settled by arithmetic. On a wallpaper whose lower half is already
      dark — like the emulator's — the 35% floor is doing nothing visible except muting
      faint texture, and it is tempting to drop it to 20%. Don't: the floor exists for the
      wallpaper that FIGHTS the theme (a white photo under the dark theme), where it is
      the only thing holding the labels above 3:1. 20% measures 1.61:1 there. The cost is
      paid on friendly wallpapers so the app is never unreadable on a hostile one.
    -->
    <color name="homeWashTopOnLight">#9EFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="homeWashFloorOnLight">#72FFFFFF</color>

    <color name="homeWashTopOnDark">#9E000000</color>
    <color name="homeWashFloorOnDark">#72000000</color>
</resources>
